</ul><h1>Almost Flame-ous</h1><p>Some years ago, I worked part-time at a local grocery store, in the wine &amp; spirits department. And it was during one of my shifts that an older woman came in and asked me where she could find champagne.</p>
<p>I showed her our selection, she chose a bottle, and I inquired if there was anything else I could help her with. “Yes,” she said. “I need a corkscrew.”</p>
<p>So I led her to where we had a few options and left her browsing while I went about my duties. But a moment later, something occurred to me.</p>
<p>I quickly tracked the woman down and said, “Ma’am, sorry I didn’t think of this before, but you really shouldn’t use a corkscrew on sparkling wine.”</p>
<p>The woman rolled her eyes and said something along the lines, “Oh, but it’s just hard to get them out sometimes.”</p>
<p>And I said something along the lines of, “No, ma’am, you don’t understand. The bottle could <em>explode</em>.”</p>
<p>We went back and forth this way for a moment more, but she remained incredulous and purchased a corkscrew anyway.</p>
<p>I think about that interaction from time to time, hoping against hope that she had a good first aid kit at home.</p>
<p>Why is this relevant? To show the limits of intuition.</p>
<p>A corkscrew, you’d think, is a screw for uncorking corked bottles. Except when said bottles’ contents are highly carbonated.</p>
<p>Likewise, when you see a fire, you likely think, <em>Let me pour some water on this thing.</em> And that works. Most of the time. Unless it’s a grease fire, in which case, you’ve just made a bad situation much worse.</p>
<p>But the two situations differ in this one important way. In the first scenario, the case of sparkling wine, it’s better to have <em>no</em> equipment on hand, lest you’re tempted to use it. With a grease fire, meanwhile, the more equipment you have within reach, the better. Having a fire extinguisher or one of these Prepared Hero fire sprays nearby, for example, means you’re less likely to run to the faucet.</p>
<p>Or, hey, why not have a fire extinguisher <em>and</em> these things? In the kitchen and in the other rooms of your house as well.</p>
